.TH OTPME-TOKEN 1 "2026-10-03" "OTPme 0.3.0a279" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-token \- manage OTPme authentication tokens
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-token
[\fB\-\-type\fR \fItoken_type\fR]
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fItoken\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B otpme-token
manages authentication tokens in the OTPme system. Tokens are credentials assigned to users. They are identified by their path:
.IR user / token_name
(e.g. alice/login).
.SH TOKEN TYPES
The type of a token is chosen when it is added and never changes:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-token \-\-type \fItype\fB add \fIuser\fB/\fIname\fR
.RE
.PP
It is not the same as the smartcard type further down: the token type says how OTPme checks a login, the smartcard type says which device a token is deployed to.
.TP
.B password
A static password.
.TP
.B hotp
One\-time password by counter (RFC 4226).
.TP
.B totp
One\-time password by time (RFC 6238), what the authenticator apps use.
.TP
.B motp
Mobile OTP, an older scheme for phones without an app store.
.TP
.B ssh
An SSH public key.
.TP
.B fido2
A hardware security key, e.g. a YubiKey or a Nitrokey.
It signs a challenge, so nothing that can be typed is enough to sign in, and the signature is bound to the site that asked \(em which is what makes it phishing resistant.
.br
Next to
.B passkey
this is the type where OTPme keeps the credential and hands it to the key at every login, and where the key is not asked to verify the user.
The login itself looks the same: name first, then the key.
.TP
.B passkey
The same thing without the extra piece of hardware: the device the user already has keeps the credential.
That is usually a phone or a notebook, where the fingerprint sensor or the face camera does the checking, and where the credential can be synced to the other devices of that user.
A hardware key can hold one as well.
.br
The credential is kept by the authenticator, together with who it belongs to, and the authenticator always asks for the finger, the face or the PIN before it signs.
So a passkey carries both factors in one step.
.br
The user name is still typed first: the login asks OTPme which credentials that user has and offers only those.
What a passkey saves is the password, not the name.
.br
Registered from the SSO portal, see
.BR otpme (7).
.TP
.B u2f
The predecessor of FIDO2, for keys that speak nothing newer.
.TP
.B yubikey_hmac
A YubiKey that answers a challenge with HMAC\-SHA1.
It works offline, which is what a notebook needs that is away from the realm.
.TP
.B yubikey_piv
A YubiKey PIV applet that holds the user's sign and encrypt keys.
This is the type OTPme recommends: the keys are made on the token and stay there, which is what encrypted shares, script and token signing, and 802.1X port authentication build on, see
.BR otpme (7).
.TP
.B tiqr
Smartphone challenge-response via the tiqr app (OCRA, RFC 6287). The
browser shows a QR code, the phone answers it over its own connection,
and the browser is signed in without anything being typed. Enrolled
from the SSO portal, not with
.BR "otpme-token add" ;
see
.BR otpme-site (1)
for the site settings
.RB ( tiqr_secret ", " sso_allow_tiqr_deploy ", " tiqr_challenge_expiry )
and
.BR otpme-user (1)
for
.BR default_sso_token_name .
Note that tiqr is not phishing resistant: nothing binds the response to
the browser that asked, so a relayed challenge yields the attacker a
session. Use FIDO2 where that matters.
.TP
.B otp_push
A one\-time password OTPme makes and pushes to the user, e.g. by mail or SMS.
The script that delivers it is
.B default_otp_push_script
in
.BR otpme (7).
.br
Not finished: where the push is sent to is still a value on the token, not the phone number of the user.
.TP
.B script_otp
A one\-time password that a script of yours checks, not OTPme.
.TP
.B script_static
The same for a static password.
.TP
.B link
A token that points at another token, named as \fIuser\fB/\fIname\fR.
Whoever signs in with it uses the credential of that token, which is how one person's key or password works for a second account.
.br
Not every type may be the destination: \fBpassword\fR, \fBhotp\fR, \fBtotp\fR, \fBmotp\fR, \fBssh\fR, \fBfido2\fR, \fByubikey_hmac\fR, \fByubikey_piv\fR, \fBscript_otp\fR and \fBscript_static\fR may, the others may not.
A \fBpasskey\fR, a \fBu2f\fR token and a \fBtiqr\fR phone are bound to their owner when they are registered, and a link to a link would only chain.
.SS Smartcard Types
These say which device a token is deployed to, and they are what the
.B deploy
command works with: \fBfido2\fR, \fBu2f\fR, \fByubikey_hmac\fR, \fByubikey_hotp\fR, \fByubikey_gpg\fR and \fByubikey_piv\fR.
.PP
Three of them share a name with a token type, and two do not:
.B yubikey_hotp
deploys a \fBhotp\fR token to the OATH applet of a YubiKey, and
.B yubikey_gpg
deploys an \fBssh\fR token to its GPG applet.
Neither is a value for \fB\-\-type\fR.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Token Management
.TP
.B \-\-type \fItype\fB add \fR[\fB\-r\fR] \fIuser\fB/\fIname\fR
Add a token to a user.
.br
\fB\-\-type\fR comes before the command, and it has to: the type decides which arguments and options \fBadd\fR takes, and OTPme reads it before it parses the rest of the line.
\fB\-r\fR replaces a token of that name and keeps its UUID, which is what a setup run does when it runs again.
.br
What each type adds to the line:
.RS
.TP
.B link
\fBadd \fIuser\fB/\fIname\fB \fIdest_user\fB/\fIdest_name\fR \(em the token it points at.
.TP
.B ssh
\fBadd \fIuser\fB/\fIname\fR [\fIpublic_key\fR] \(em without a key the token is added and the key set later.
.TP
.B password
\fB\-\-password\fR \fIpassword\fR sets it right away, \fB\-\-weak\-password\fR takes one the password policy would refuse, and \fB\-\-enable\-mschap\fR keeps the NT hash on the server so that MSCHAP logins work.
.TP
.BR hotp " and " totp
\fB\-\-mode\fR \fImode\fR and \fB\-\-no\-qrcode\fR, see below.
.RE
.IP
The other types take nothing beyond the token path.
.RS
.IP "\fB\-\-mode\fR \fImode\fR"
Token mode. Defaults to
.BR mode2 .
In
.B mode2
neither the OTP secret nor the PIN are stored in the token object; the
effective OTP secret is derived from the server-side secret together with the
PIN. This mode is compatible with the SSO portal, but changing the PIN
requires redeploying the token (a new QR code must be scanned).
In
.B mode1
the OTP secret is stored directly in the token object, so PIN changes do not
require redeployment.
.IP \fB\-\-no\-qrcode\fR
Do not display the QR code on token creation.
.RE
.TP
.B del \fR[\fB\-\-no\-trash\fR] \fItoken\fR
Delete a token.
It goes to the trash, from where
.BR otpme\-trash (1)
can bring it back; \fB\-\-no\-trash\fR deletes it right away.
.TP
.B show \fR[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-z\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-fields\fR \fIF1,F2\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-role\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-scope\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] \fR[\fItoken\fR]
Show the tokens, with their roles, scopes and policies.
.br
Without a token you get your own, i.e. the tokens of the user you are signed in as.
.RS
.TP
.B \-a
Also the tokens you may only look at, not just the ones you may change.
.TP
.B \-z \fIN\fR
How many tokens to print.
Without it you get 10.
.TP
.B \-\-fields \fIF1,F2\fR
Print these columns and no others, in the order they are named.
The names are the ones in the header of the table.
.br
A name with a \fB+\fR in front is added to the columns that are shown anyway, and one with a \fB\-\fR is left out of them.
The two cannot be mixed with a plain list.
.br
Two columns are not in the table unless they are asked for:
.B authscript
and
.BR inherit .
.RS
.PP
.nf
\&otpme-token show \-\-fields +authscript,+inherit
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR
How many roles, scopes and policies to print per token.
Without it you get 5 of each, or 30 of each when you asked for one token by name.
.TP
.B \-\-role\-limit \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-scope\-limit \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-policy\-limit \fIN\fR
The same for one of the three, where the others should stay as they are.
.RE
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB\-a\fR] \fR[\fIregex\fR]
List the token paths, and with a regex only those that match it, e.g. \fBalice/*\fR for the tokens of one user.
.br
You get the tokens you may change; \fB\-a\fR adds those you may only look at.
.TP
.B enable \fR[\fB\-\-share\-notify\fR|\fB\-\-no\-share\-notify\fR] \fR[\fB\-\-no\-persist\-mount\fR] \fItoken\fR
Enable a disabled token.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-share\-notify
Send notification to all online hosts on new share permissions.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-share\-notify
Do not send notification to all online hosts on new share permissions.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-persist\-mount
Do not persist new share mounts on hosts.
.RE
.TP
.B disable \fR[\fB\-\-share\-notify\fR|\fB\-\-no\-share\-notify\fR] \fR[\fB\-\-no\-persist\-mount\fR] \fItoken\fR
Disable a token without deleting it.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-share\-notify
Send notification to all online hosts on revoked share permissions.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-share\-notify
Do not send notification to all online hosts on revoked share permissions.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-persist\-mount
Keep persisted share mounts on hosts (transient unmount only).
.RE
.TP
.B rename \fItoken\fR \fInew_name\fR
Rename a token.
.br
The default token of a user is only renamed where the config parameter
.B allow_default_token_rename
says so, see
.BR otpme (7).
.TP
.B move \fR[\fB\-r\fR] \fItoken\fR \fIdst_user\fR[\fB/\fIname\fR]
Move a token to another user.
The user has to be on the same site; moving a token to another site does not work.
.br
\fB\-r\fR replaces a token of that name at the destination and keeps its UUID.
Without it OTPme asks, and it says so when the token it would replace is the default token of that user.
.br
Moving into the
.B TOKENSTORE
gives the token a name of its own, \fIold_owner\fB:\fIname\fR, with a number appended where that is taken already.
A name of your own is refused there.
Moving it back out again takes the part behind the \fB:\fR as the name, unless you give one.
.TP
.B touch \fItoken\fR
Re-index the object to fix potential index problems.
.SS Token Configuration
.TP
.B config \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] \fItoken\fR \fIparameter\fR \fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Set a configuration parameter on the token.
\fB\-a\fR appends the value to a parameter that holds a list.
.br
\fB\-d\fR removes the parameter from the token, or with a value only that one value from a list.
What the token gets afterwards is whatever the user, the unit or the site say.
See \fBCONFIG PARAMETERS\fR in
.BR otpme (7).
.TP
.B show_config \fItoken\fR [\fIparameter\fR]
Show the parameters set on the token itself, or only the given one.
.TP
.B get_config \fItoken\fR \fIparameter\fR
Show the value of one parameter the way OTPme reads it, i.e. after looking at the token, the user, the unit and the site.
So this answers what applies, while
.B show_config
answers what was set here.
.TP
.B auto_disable \fR[\fB\-u\fR] \fItoken\fR \fItime\fR
Set auto\-disable time.
It is either a relative time with a leading \fB+\fR, e.g. \fB+30D\fR, or an absolute one, e.g. \fB"09:53 13.06.2023"\fR.
The units are \fBs\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBh\fR, \fBD\fR, \fBW\fR, \fBM\fR and \fBY\fR, see
.BR otpme (7).
Use \fB\-u\fR to disable the token if it was unused for the given time.
.TP
.B description \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIdescription\fR]
Set the description of the token.
Without one OTPme asks and offers the current text for editing.
.TP
.B device_name \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIdevice_name\fR]
Set the name of the device this token lives on, e.g. the phone or the security key it was registered with.
.br
The SSO portal asks the user for it when they add a token there and shows it in the list, so that somebody with three security keys can tell them apart.
.TP
.B info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIinfo\fR]
Set the info text of the token.
Without a text OTPme opens the current one in the editor named by
.BR EDITOR .
.br
An info text is kept per language.
\fB\-\-language\fR says which one; without it the text is stored under \fBinfo\fR, which is the note of the administrator.
.TP
.B dump_info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fItoken\fR
Write the info text of that language to stdout.
.TP
.B test \fItoken\fR
Check whether the token verifies.
OTPme asks for the OTP or the password; it is never given on the command line.
.br
It is the real verification, not a dry run: an OTP tested here is spent, and the counter of a
.B hotp
token moves on.
.br
Almost every type has it.
.B passkey
and
.B tiqr
do not: both need a browser and a device that answers it, which a command line cannot stand in for.
.TP
.B temp_password \fR[\fB\-\-generate\fR] [\fB\-\-duration\fR \fItime\fR] [\fB\-\-remove\fR] [\fB\-\-weak\-password\fR] \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIpassword\fR]
Give the token a second password that works for a while, next to the one it has.
It is meant for an administrator or the IT support who has to work as that user for a time, e.g. to set up a notebook.
.br
\fB\-\-generate\fR makes one up, \fB\-\-duration\fR says how long it lives (\fB1h\fR without it, time units as in \fBotpme\fR(7)), and \fB\-\-remove\fR takes it away again.
.br
Setting one needs the config parameter
.B allow_temp_passwords
and the ACL \fBset_temp_password\fR, on the token or on the user above it.
Removing one always works.
On a \fBlink\fR token the password is set on the token it points at.
.br
\fB\-\-weak\-password\fR accepts a password the password policy rejects, which needs the \fBforce_password\fR ACL.
This is not the same as \fB\-f\fR, which only skips the confirmation.
.SS VLAN Assignment
A token has no VLAN command of its own.
The way to give it one is to put it, or a role it is in, into the VLAN:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme-vlan add_token \fIvlan\fR \fIuser\fB/\fItoken\fR
.RE
.PP
That is the assignment that counts.
A VLAN of the site answering the RADIUS request that names the token, or a role the token is in, decides the answer, and nothing below is asked any more.
The nearer assignment wins: a VLAN naming the token itself beats one naming its role, see
.BR otpme\-vlan (1).
.PP
Only where no such VLAN names the token does OTPme ask the
.B vlans
config parameter (see
.BR otpme (7)):
.PP
.RS
.B otpme-token config \fIuser\fB/\fItoken\fR vlans \fIvlan\fR
.RE
.PP
It is read along the cascade of the token: first on the token, then on the user, then on the unit, then on the site.
.PP
The VLAN goes out with the answer of an 802.1X port authentication.
.SS Offline Configuration
A host may keep a copy of a token so that the user can still log in while the realm cannot be reached \(em the notebook on the road.
The copy is encrypted on the host.
.PP
Where the key for it comes from depends on the token.
A password or OTP token derives it from what the user types, with the hash of
.B offline_token_hash_type
and the options of
.BR pam_otpme (8).
A smartcard derives it on the card: the host sends a stored challenge plus the password to the card, and the answer of the card is the key.
So a copy of a smartcard token is worth nothing without the card.
.TP
.B enable_offline \fItoken\fR
Allow the hosts to keep this token.
.br
Not every type can: \fBotp_push\fR, \fBlink\fR, \fBscript_otp\fR and \fBscript_static\fR answer "Offline usage not supported by this token".
All the others can, and start with it switched off.
.TP
.B disable_offline \fItoken\fR
Do not keep it any more.
.TP
.B offline_expiry \fItoken\fR \fItime\fR
How long a copy is good for, counted from the last login that reached the realm.
.br
\fB0\fR, the value a token starts with, means it never expires.
Time units as in
.BR otpme (7).
.TP
.B offline_unused_expiry \fItoken\fR \fItime\fR
The same, counted from the last time the copy was used.
\fB0\fR again means never.
.br
Both are checked on the host, and only while nobody of that user is logged in; what is past its time is deleted.
.SS Session
.TP
.B enable_session_keep \fItoken\fR
Keep the login session of this token over a reboot.
.br
The host writes the session key into the offline copy of the token, so the session is taken up again instead of the user logging in anew.
It needs the offline copy, see \fBOffline Configuration\fR above.
.br
\fBotp_push\fR, \fBscript_otp\fR and \fBscript_static\fR cannot do it and answer "Session keeping not supported by this token".
The other types can, and start with it switched off.
.TP
.B disable_session_keep \fItoken\fR
Do not keep the session any more.
.SS Auth Script
.TP
.B auth_script \fItoken\fR \fIscript\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fR[\fIscript_options\fR]
Set the authorization script of the token.
.br
The script runs at every login with this token, after the credential was checked, and it can still refuse the login.
How it gets its values is the same as for the user scripts, see \fBUser Scripts\fR in
.BR otpme\-user (1).
.TP
.B enable_auth_script \fItoken\fR
Run that script.
.TP
.B disable_auth_script \fItoken\fR
Do not run it.
.br
The three commands do nothing for a \fBscript_otp\fR or \fBscript_static\fR token: there the script \fIis\fR the check, and there is nothing to switch on top of it.
.SS Deploy
A hardware token must be deployed before it can be used. Deploy sets up the device and creates the matching OTPme token. Name the smartcard type with \fB\-\-type\fR before the command:
.PP
.RS 4
otpme\-token \-\-type yubikey_piv deploy john/piv
.RE
.PP
The type decides which options the command takes, so the options differ per type. The token name is always needed and is always \fIuser/token\fR.
.TP
.B deploy \fB\-\-list\-token\-types\fR
List the smartcard types that can be deployed. See
.B Smartcard Types
above for the token type each one creates.
.SS Deploy \- FIDO2
.TP
.B deploy \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-pin\fR] [\fB\-\-uv\fR \fIuv\fR] \fItoken\fR
Register a FIDO2 key. The key must be plugged in and asks for a touch.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pin
Do not set a PIN on the key. This only matters when the key supports a PIN but has none yet. Then deploy would ask for a new one. A PIN that is already set is never changed.
.TP
.B \-\-uv \fIuv\fR
User verification: \fBdiscouraged\fR, \fBpreferred\fR or \fBrequired\fR. Without this option OTPme asks the key. It takes \fBrequired\fR when the key supports a PIN, \fBpreferred\fR when the key does user verification or biometrics, else \fBdiscouraged\fR. See also the
.B uv
command below.
.TP
.B \-r
Replace existing token (keep UUID).
.TP
.B \-d
Enable debug output.
.SS Deploy \- U2F
.TP
.B deploy \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] \fItoken\fR
Register a U2F key. The key must be plugged in and asks for a touch. U2F knows no PIN and no user verification, so \fB\-\-no\-pin\fR and \fB\-\-uv\fR do not exist here.
.TP
.B \-r
Replace existing token (keep UUID).
.TP
.B \-d
Enable debug output.
.SS Deploy \- YubiKey HMAC\-SHA1
.TP
.B deploy \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIslot\fR] \fItoken\fR
Write an HMAC\-SHA1 secret to a YubiKey slot. Deploy asks for a token password. The token secret is the card's answer to a challenge, so the secret of the card itself never leaves it. Whatever was in the slot is lost. Deploy asks before it writes, unless you use the global \fB\-f\fR.
.TP
.B \-s \fIslot\fR
Slot to use. Default is slot 2.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not write to the YubiKey. Use the secret that is already in the slot. This works because OTPme only needs the answer to a challenge, not the secret. Use it for a card that is already set up, or to give a second OTPme token the same card.
.TP
.B \-r
Replace existing token (keep UUID).
.TP
.B \-d
Enable debug output.
.SS Deploy \- YubiKey OATH HOTP
.TP
.B deploy \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIslot\fR] \fItoken\fR
Write an OATH HOTP secret to a YubiKey slot. Deploy asks for a token PIN. The PIN and a server secret together make the secret on the card, so the user types the PIN before the OTP. Whatever was in the slot is lost. Deploy asks before it writes, unless you use the global \fB\-f\fR. Re\-plug the card when deploy asks for it.
.TP
.B \-s \fIslot\fR
Slot to use. Default is slot 1.
.TP
.B \-r
Replace existing token (keep UUID).
.TP
.B \-d
Enable debug output.
.PP
There is no \fB\-n\fR here. The secret cannot be read back from the card, so OTPme can only register a card it wrote itself.
.SS Deploy \- YubiKey GPG Applet
.TP
.B deploy \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-\-key\-len\fR \fIbits\fR] [\fB\-\-backup\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-restore\fR \fIfile\fR] \fItoken\fR
Set up the GPG applet of a YubiKey and create an \fBssh\fR token from it. This resets the applet, so every GPG key on the card is lost.
.PP
Deploy asks for your real name, your mail address and one token password of at least 8 characters. That password becomes the user PIN and the Admin PIN of the card.
.PP
Deploy creates an RSA master key and three subkeys, one per card slot: signature, encryption and authentication. The master key stays in the backup file, the three subkeys go to the card. The SSH public key of the token is the authentication key.
.PP
Before the keys go to the card, deploy writes a backup of them. Default path: \fI/dev/shm/<real_name>.gpg\fR, with spaces replaced by underscores. Keep that file. Without it the keys are gone when the card breaks.
.PP
Deploy refuses to start when a gpg\-agent is running or when \fI~/.gnupg\fR exists. It needs its own GPG home directory.
.TP
.B \-\-key\-len \fIbits\fR
RSA key length of the master key and of all three subkeys. Allowed: 2048, 3072 (default) and 4096. The keys are created on the host and then moved to the card, so the card has to accept the length. Old YubiKeys only take 2048.
.TP
.B \-\-backup \fIfile\fR
Write the key backup to \fIfile\fR instead of the default path.
.TP
.B \-\-restore \fIfile\fR
Take the keys from a backup \fIfile\fR instead of creating new ones. Use this to move a token to a new card. \fB\-\-key\-len\fR does nothing here, the keys come from the file. Deploy then asks for the passphrase of the backup file and for a new token password. Cannot be used together with \fB\-\-backup\fR.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not touch the card. Read the SSH public key from a card whose GPG applet is already set up and only register the token in OTPme.
.TP
.B \-r
Replace existing token (keep UUID).
.TP
.B \-d
Enable debug output.
.SS Deploy \- YubiKey PIV Applet
.TP
.B deploy \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-\-key\-len\fR \fIbits\fR] [\fB\-\-sign\-algo\fR \fIalgo\fR] [\fB\-\-encrypt\-algo\fR \fIalgo\fR] [\fB\-\-backup\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-restore\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-restore\-from\-server\fR] [\fB\-\-backup\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-add\-user\-key\fR] \fItoken\fR
Set up the PIV applet of a YubiKey. Deploy creates two keys: a sign key in slot 9A and an encrypt key in slot 9D. It asks for a smartcard password of at least 8 characters.
.PP
Where the key backup goes depends on the config parameter \fBprivate_key_backup_key\fR of the site or unit:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Not set: deploy writes a local backup file. Default path: \fI/dev/shm/<user>_<token>.json\fR. The file holds both private keys as PEM in a JSON file. Both are encrypted, with the token password or with a second password deploy asks for.
.IP \(bu 2
Set: deploy sends the backup to the server, encrypted with that public key. No local file is written and \fB\-\-backup\fR does nothing. Get the keys back with \fB\-\-restore\-from\-server\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-key\-len \fIbits\fR
RSA key length. Allowed: 2048 (default), 3072 and 4096. A YubiKey needs firmware 5.7 or newer for 3072 and 4096. Only used when the algorithm is RSA.
.TP
.B \-\-sign\-algo \fIalgo\fR
Algorithm for the sign slot 9A: \fBrsa\fR (default) or \fBed25519\fR. Ed25519 needs YubiKey firmware 5.7 or newer.
.TP
.B \-\-encrypt\-algo \fIalgo\fR
Algorithm for the encrypt slot 9D: \fBrsa\fR (default) or \fBx25519\fR. X25519 needs YubiKey firmware 5.7 or newer.
.TP
.B \-\-backup \fIfile\fR
Write the key backup to \fIfile\fR instead of the default path.
.TP
.B \-\-restore \fIfile\fR
Take the keys from a backup \fIfile\fR instead of creating new ones. Cannot be used together with \fB\-\-backup\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-from\-server
Take the keys from the server backup of an existing token. Needs \fB\-\-backup\-key\-file\fR. Cannot be used together with \fB\-n\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-backup\-key\-file \fIfile\fR
Private key that decrypts the server backup. This is the key file the client wrote when \fBprivate_key_backup_key\fR was set on the site or unit.
.TP
.B \-\-add\-user\-key
Use the token keys as the user's keys. This sets the sign and the encrypt public key of the user and makes this token the key token of the user. See
.BR otpme\-user (1).
.TP
.B \-n
Do not touch the card. Read both public keys from a card whose PIV applet is already set up and only register the token in OTPme.
.TP
.B \-r
Replace existing token (keep UUID).
.TP
.B \-d
Enable debug output.
.SS Dynamic Groups
A dynamic group is a local group on the host, not an OTPme group. At login pam_otpme puts the user into it for that session. See
.BR otpme (7)
for the whole picture.
.PP
Who logs in with this token gets these groups. You can also set dynamic groups on a role, then every token of the role gets them.
.TP
.B add_dynamic_group \fItoken\fR \fIgroup\fR
Add a dynamic group to the token. The name must not be an OTPme group. Needs the ACL \fBadd:dynamic_group\fR. Asks before it changes anything, the global \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B remove_dynamic_group \fItoken\fR \fIgroup\fR
Remove a dynamic group from the token. Needs the ACL \fBremove:dynamic_group\fR.
.TP
.B list_dynamic_groups \fR[\fB\-\-include\-roles\fR] \fItoken\fR
List the dynamic groups of the token. Needs the ACL \fBview:dynamic_groups\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-roles
Also list the dynamic groups of the roles of the token. This is the list a login gets, minus the groups of the host.
.SS Listing
A command that starts with \fBlist_\fR answers what is assigned to the token. One that starts with \fBfind_\fR answers the other question: where is this token used. Nothing is assigned to a token except its policies and its dynamic groups, so almost everything here is a \fBfind_\fR.
.PP
\fB\-\-return\-type\fR picks what they print: \fBname\fR (default), \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR or \fBuuid\fR. A name from another site is printed as \fIsite/name\fR.
.PP
A token reaches most of what it has through its roles. These commands show what the token itself is assigned to. Where there is a \fB\-\-include\-roles\fR, that is how you ask for the rest.
.TP
.B find_roles \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-r\fR] \fItoken\fR
Find the roles of the token. With \fB\-r\fR also the roles those roles are in. Needs the ACL \fBfind:roles\fR.
.TP
.B find_groups \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-include\-roles\fR] \fItoken\fR
Find the groups of the token. With \fB\-\-include\-roles\fR also the groups the token is in through its roles. Needs the ACL \fBfind:groups\fR.
.TP
.B find_accessgroups \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-include\-roles\fR] \fItoken\fR
Find the access groups of the token. With \fB\-\-include\-roles\fR also the ones it reaches through its roles. Needs the ACL \fBfind:accessgroups\fR.
.TP
.B find_scopes \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-include\-roles\fR] \fItoken\fR
Find the OIDC scopes of the token. With \fB\-\-include\-roles\fR also the ones it reaches through its roles. Needs the ACL \fBfind:scopes\fR.
.TP
.B find_shares \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] \fItoken\fR
Find the shares the token may use. The roles of the token are always included here, there is nothing to switch. \fB\-\-return\-type\fR also takes \fBpath\fR, which is the share name with its site in front. Needs the ACL \fBfind:shares\fR.
.TP
.B find_hosts \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] \fItoken\fR
Find the hosts the token is assigned to. Only direct assignments, the roles are not followed. Needs the ACL \fBfind:hosts\fR.
.TP
.B find_nodes \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] \fItoken\fR
Find the nodes the token is assigned to. Only direct assignments, the roles are not followed. Needs the ACL \fBfind:nodes\fR.
.TP
.B find_acls \fItoken\fR
Find the ACLs this token holds on other objects. Not the ACLs set on the token, those are
.BR list_acls .
Only an admin may run this.
.SS Object Changelog
Every change to the object writes an entry: who made it, when, and what was done.
A change can carry a comment of its own, through the global option \fB\-\-changelog\fR.
.PP
An \fIentry_id\fR is the number the listing shows in front of an entry, counted from 0.
.PP
Only the comment of an entry can be changed or removed.
What was done and by whom stays as it is.
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B changelog \fItoken\fR
Show the changelog of the token.
Needs the ACL \fBview:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B edit_changelog \fItoken\fR \fIentry_id\fR \fIcomment\fR
Set the comment of that entry, or replace the one it has.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B del_changelog \fItoken\fR \fIentry_id\fR
Remove the comment of that entry.
The entry itself stays, and an entry without a comment is refused.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B clear_changelog \fItoken\fR
Remove every entry of the changelog.
.br
This needs more than the ACL \fBclear:changelog\fR: the config parameter
.B allow_changelog_clearing
has to allow it (see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
It is true by default, but where no value is found at all, clearing is refused.
OTPme asks the token you are signed in with first, then the object.
.SS Policy Management
A \fIpolicy\fR is the name of a policy, not a path.
It is looked for in the site of the token.
Which policies there are, and what each of them does, is in
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
.PP
Attaching and detaching ask before they change anything; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_policy \fItoken\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Attach a policy to the token.
.br
A policy has a list of object types it is for; one that does not name \fBtoken\fR is refused.
So is a second policy of a type that allows only one per object.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBassign:policy\fR.
.TP
.B remove_policy \fItoken\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Remove a policy from the token.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:policy\fR, and that one verbatim: a plain \fBremove\fR does not cover it.
Detaching a policy takes away what it enforces, so it must not be cheaper than attaching one.
.TP
.B list_policies \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fItoken\fR
List the policies attached to the token.
Use \fB\-\-return\-type\fR to select the attribute returned (\fBname\fR, \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR, \fBuuid\fR).
Use \fB\-\-policy\-types\fR to list only policies of the given types; these are policy types, not policy names.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:policies\fR.
.SS ACL Management
An ACL says what somebody may do with this token.
The somebody is a role or a token, never a user.
.PP
What the three forms \fIacl\fR, \fB+\fIacl\fR and \fB++\fIacl\fR mean, and which ACLs exist, is under \fBACLs\fR in
.BR otpme (7).
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_acl \fItoken\fR \fIowner_type\fR \fIowner\fR \fIacl\fR[\fB:\fIvalue\fR]
Give a role or a token an ACL on this token.
.br
\fIowner_type\fR is \fBrole\fR or \fBtoken\fR, and \fIowner\fR the path of that role or token.
An ACL may carry a value, for example \fBview:token_type\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:acl\fR.
.TP
.B del_acl \fItoken\fR \fIacl\fR
Take that ACL away again.
.br
Here the \fIacl\fR is the whole entry, owner and all, the way \fBlist_acls\fR prints it: \fBrole:berlin/sw\-admins:set_temp_password\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdelete:acl\fR.
.TP
.B list_acls \fItoken\fR
Show the ACLs that lie on this token, i.e. who may do what with it.
Needs the ACL \fBview:acl\fR.
.TP
.B find_acls \fItoken\fR
The other direction: the ACLs this token holds on other objects.
.br
Admin only. This one cannot be given away.
.TP
.B enable_acl_inheritance \fItoken\fR
Let the token take the ACLs that reach it from elsewhere. It does by default.
.RS
.PP
Two things reach it: a default ACL of the unit the token sits in, and an ACL added to its user with \fB\-r\fR (see
.BR otpme\-user (1)).
A token that does not inherit is passed over by both.
.PP
Only default ACLs are inherited. An ordinary ACL on the unit never is.
.PP
Moving a token into another unit is the one place that asks something else as well: an admin token gets neither the default ACLs of the new unit nor loses those of the old one.
That is the admin token of the site and every token in the site admin role \fBSITE_ADMIN\fR.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBenable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B disable_acl_inheritance \fItoken\fR
Stop that.
Only the ACLs on the token itself count from then on.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdisable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.SS Export and Cleanup
Everything here writes to stdout or reads a file.
There is no import on this page: reading an export back in is
.BR otpme\-tool " " import ,
see
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.PP
\fBset_token_data\fR and \fBremove_orphans\fR ask before they change anything; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B export \fR[\fB\-\-password\fR \fIPASS\fR] \fItoken\fR
Write the whole object of the token to stdout, as JSON.
.br
Without \fB\-\-password\fR it is written as it is, in clear.
With one, a key is derived from that password and the object is encrypted with it; the salt goes into the output, so the password is all you need to read it back.
.br
This is the object, so it holds what the token logs in with.
An export without a password is as secret as the token itself.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBexport\fR.
.TP
.B dump_token_data \fItoken\fR
Write what the token logs in with to stdout, as JSON. Not the whole object, only that.
.RS
.PP
What it is depends on the type:
the secret for \fBhotp\fR, \fBtotp\fR, \fBmotp\fR and \fBtiqr\fR,
the password hashes for \fBpassword\fR and \fBotp_push\fR,
the public key for \fBssh\fR,
the credential for \fBfido2\fR, \fBpasskey\fR and \fBu2f\fR,
the slot and the secret for \fByubikey_hmac\fR.
.PP
It is what the server keeps, never what is on a card.
A \fByubikey_piv\fR token writes its two public keys and its 802.1X secret; the private keys stay on the card.
.PP
Every token type can do this but \fBlink\fR, \fBscript_otp\fR and \fBscript_static\fR.
.PP
For every type that logs in with a shared secret, whoever reads this can build a second token that logs in the same way.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBview:token_data\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B set_token_data \fItoken\fR \fIfile\fR
Load that data into a token. \fIfile\fR holds what \fBdump_token_data\fR wrote.
.RS
.PP
Use it to keep a user logging in with the device they have when the token object has to be replaced.
.PP
The type is not checked.
The dump does not say which type it came from, and loading sets only the fields the type of \fItoken\fR knows.
A dump from another type sets the fields that happen to carry the same name, leaves the rest as it was, and still reports success.
\fBhotp\fR and \fBtotp\fR have the same three fields, so either dump loads into either token.
Make sure yourself that both tokens are of the same type.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBedit:token_data\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B remove_orphans \fItoken\fR
Remove what the token still points at although it is gone.
.RS
.PP
On a token that is its ACLs, its policies and its signatures.
An ACL for a role that was deleted is one, and so is a signature of a user that is gone.
.PP
It shows everything it found and asks once for all of it.
It asks nothing and changes nothing when it finds nothing.
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBremove:orphans\fR.
.RE
.SH TOKEN TYPE COMMANDS
Each type has commands of its own.
They need \fB\-\-type\fR in front of the command, because the name of a command says nothing about the type:
.PP
.RS
.B otpme\-token \-\-type hotp resync alice/phone
.RE
.PP
A type that is not named below has no commands of its own.
.SS OTP Tokens (hotp, totp, motp)
.TP
.B secret \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIsecret\fR]
Set the secret the OTPs are made from.
Without a \fIsecret\fR a new one is made.
.br
The same secret sits on both sides: the token makes an OTP from it and OTPme makes the same OTP to compare.
So changing it here stops every copy of the token from working until it has the new one as well.
.br
For \fBhotp\fR and \fBtotp\fR it is base32, which is what the authenticator apps read.
.TP
.B show_secret \fItoken\fR
Show that secret.
In \fBmode2\fR it asks for the PIN first, because the secret cannot be read without it.
.TP
.B pin \fR[\fB\-\-generate\fR] \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIpin\fR]
Set the PIN the user types in front of the OTP.
.B \-\-generate
makes one.
.TP
.B show_pin \fItoken\fR
Show the PIN.
Not in \fBmode2\fR: it is not stored there.
.TP
.B enable_pin \fItoken\fR / \fBdisable_pin \fItoken\fR
Ask for a PIN, or stop asking.
\fBhotp\fR and \fBtotp\fR only, and not in \fBmode2\fR, where the PIN is part of the secret.
An \fBmotp\fR always has one.
.TP
.B gen \fItoken\fR
Make an OTP here, for testing.
In \fBmode2\fR it asks for the PIN.
.TP
.B gen_mschap \fItoken\fR
Make an MSCHAP challenge and response pair from an OTP of this token.
For testing a RADIUS setup without a client.
.TP
.B gen_qrcode \fItoken\fR
Print a QR code an authenticator app can read.
\fBhotp\fR and \fBtotp\fR only.
.TP
.B mode \fItoken\fR \fImode\fR
Change where the secret lives.
\fBhotp\fR and \fBtotp\fR only.
.RS
.PP
In
.B mode1
OTPme holds the whole secret.
A PIN is optional, and OTPme checks it itself.
.PP
In
.B mode2
OTPme holds half of it.
The secret of the token is made from what OTPme holds and the PIN, so the PIN is needed and is stored nowhere.
A wrong PIN gives a wrong OTP, and OTPme cannot tell the two apart \(em it sees a wrong OTP and nothing more.
It also cannot make an OTP or show the secret without being given the PIN.
.RE
.SS HOTP only
.TP
.B counter_check_range \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIrange\fR]
How many counters past the last one are tried at login.
Default is the \fBhotp_check_range\fR of the site or unit, which is \fB32\fR.
.br
A counter-based token moves on every press, and a few presses that never reached OTPme are normal.
Set it too high and an OTP somebody wrote down stays good for longer.
.TP
.B resync \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIotp\fR]
Find the counter again when it ran too far ahead.
Give one OTP of the token and OTPme looks for it over \fBhotp_resync_check_range\fR counters, which is \fB1024\fR.
.TP
.B get_token_counter \fItoken\fR
The counter OTPme holds for this token.
.SS mOTP only
.TP
.B validity_time \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIminutes\fR]
How long an OTP of this token counts.
In minutes, default \fB60\fR (\fBmotp_validity_time\fR).
.TP
.B timedrift_tolerance \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIminutes\fR]
How far the clock of the phone may be off.
In minutes, default \fB15\fR (\fBmotp_timedrift_tolerance\fR).
.TP
.B enable_mschap \fItoken\fR / \fBdisable_mschap \fItoken\fR
Allow this token for MSCHAP, or stop allowing it.
.SS Password Token
.TP
.B password \fR[\fB\-\-generate\fR] [\fB\-\-weak\-password\fR] \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIpassword\fR]
Set the password.
.B \-\-generate
makes one.
.br
With
.B \-\-weak\-password
a password the password policy rejects is taken anyway.
That needs the \fBforce_password\fR ACL, and it is not what \fB\-f\fR does \(em \fB\-f\fR only skips the question.
.TP
.B upgrade_pass_hash \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIhash_type\fR] \fR[\fIargs\fR]
Hash the stored password hash again, with this hash type.
.br
The password is not needed for it, so this can be run over old tokens after the realm moved to a stronger hash.
What is stored afterwards is the old hash inside the new one, and a login still works: OTPme hashes what is typed the same way down the chain.
.TP
.B 2f_token \fItoken\fR \fIsecond_factor_token\fR
Set the token that is asked for as the second factor.
.TP
.B enable_2f \fItoken\fR / \fBdisable_2f \fItoken\fR
Ask for that second token, or stop asking.
.TP
.B enable_mschap \fItoken\fR / \fBdisable_mschap \fItoken\fR
Allow this token for MSCHAP, or stop allowing it.
.br
MSCHAP needs an NT hash of the password, so enabling it stores one.
.TP
.B remove_nt_hash \fItoken\fR
Remove that NT hash.
.TP
.B gen_mschap \fItoken\fR
Make an MSCHAP challenge and response pair from the second factor token of this token.
For testing a RADIUS setup without a client.
.TP
.B enable_sso_deploy \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIdeploy_token_type\fR]
Let the user deploy a real token for themselves at their first login on the SSO portal.
The password token is what gets them in that once.
.I deploy_token_type
picks the type they get, so they are not asked.
.TP
.B disable_sso_deploy \fItoken\fR
Stop that.
.SS SSH Token
.TP
.B ssh_public_key \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIssh_public_key\fR]
Set the public key.
A whole \fBauthorized_keys\fR line may be pasted; what is stored is the key out of it.
The type is read from the key, so \fBkey_type\fR does not have to be set first.
.TP
.B card_type \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIcard_type\fR]
Say that the private key lives on a card.
The only value is \fBgpg\fR, for the GPG applet of a YubiKey.
.TP
.B key_type \fItoken\fR \fIkey_type\fR
The kind of key: \fBrsa\fR, \fBed25519\fR or \fBecdsa\fR.
.TP
.B password \fR[\fB\-\-generate\fR] [\fB\-\-weak\-password\fR] \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIpassword\fR]
Set the password of the token.
See the password token above for \fB\-\-weak\-password\fR.
.TP
.B 2f_token \fItoken\fR \fIsecond_factor_token\fR
Set the token that is asked for as the second factor.
.TP
.B enable_2f \fItoken\fR / \fBdisable_2f \fItoken\fR
Ask for that second token, or stop asking.
.TP
.B get_sign_data \fR[\fB\-\-tags\fR \fItag1,tag2\fR] \fItoken\fR
The bytes that have to be signed, for signing somewhere else.
.PP
An SSH token can be signed, so it also takes
.BR sign ,
.BR resign ,
.BR verify_sign ,
.BR get_sign ,
.B add_sign
and
.BR del_sign .
See
.B Signing a token
below.
.SS Link Token
.TP
.B add \fItoken\fR \fIdestination_token\fR
Make the link and say which token it points at, as \fIuser\fB/\fIname\fR.
Whoever signs in with the link uses the credential of that token.
.SS YubiKey HMAC
.TP
.B secret \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIsecret\fR]
Set the HMAC secret of the key.
It is the same secret on the key itself, so
.B otpme\-token deploy
is the usual way to set it.
.TP
.B show_secret \fItoken\fR
Show that secret.
.TP
.B gen \fItoken\fR
Make an answer to the stored challenge, for testing.
.TP
.B gen_mschap \fItoken\fR
Make an MSCHAP challenge and response pair from that answer.
For testing a RADIUS setup without a client.
.TP
.B enable_mschap \fItoken\fR / \fBdisable_mschap \fItoken\fR
Allow this token for MSCHAP, or stop allowing it.
.TP
.B remove_nt_hash \fItoken\fR
Remove the NT hash used for MSCHAP.
.TP
.B validity_time \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIminutes\fR] \fRand\fB timedrift_tolerance \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIminutes\fR]
Both are stored and nothing reads them.
This token answers a challenge, so there is no OTP with a lifetime and no clock to be off.
They are only here because the type shares its commands with the OTP tokens.
.SS OTP Push Token
.TP
.B push_token \fItoken\fR \fIotp_token\fR
Which OTP token of the same user the pushed OTP is made from.
By name, not as a path, and it has to be an OTP token.
.TP
.B phone_number \fItoken\fR \fR[\fInumber\fR]
Where the OTP is sent.
.br
This is a value on the token, not the phone number of the user \(em see
.B otp_push
under
.B TOKEN TYPES
above.
.TP
.B push_script \fItoken\fR \fIscript\fR
The script that delivers it.
Without one the default of the realm is used, \fBdefault_otp_push_script\fR in
.BR otpme (7).
.TP
.B password \fR[\fB\-\-generate\fR] [\fB\-\-weak\-password\fR] \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIpassword\fR]
Set the password of the token.
See the password token above for \fB\-\-weak\-password\fR.
.TP
.B enable_mschap \fItoken\fR / \fBdisable_mschap \fItoken\fR
Allow this token for MSCHAP, or stop allowing it.
.TP
.B remove_nt_hash \fItoken\fR
Remove the NT hash used for MSCHAP.
.SS FIDO2
.TP
.B uv \fItoken\fR \fIuv\fR
Whether the key has to check the user before it signs: \fBdiscouraged\fR, \fBpreferred\fR or \fBrequired\fR.
Default is \fBpreferred\fR.
.br
With
.B required
the key asks for its PIN or the finger, and the token counts as two factors on its own.
With the other two a login is the key alone, and whoever holds the key can use it.
.SS YubiKey PIV
A PIV token has two keys, one for signing and one for encrypting.
.B otpme\-token deploy
makes both and writes them into the slots of the card, and it does everything below on its own \(em see
.B Deploy \- YubiKey PIV
above, which also says where the backup of the private keys goes.
.PP
What these commands set is the public half, which is the half OTPme keeps.
You only need them by hand when a card was set up somewhere else.
.PP
A PIV token can also be signed, see
.B Signing a token
below.
.TP
.B sign_public_key \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIpublic_key\fR]
Set the public half of the sign key.
.TP
.B encrypt_public_key \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIpublic_key\fR]
Set the public half of the encrypt key.
.TP
.B ssh_public_key \fItoken\fR \fR[\fIssh_public_key\fR]
Set the SSH public key of the card, so this token gets the user onto hosts as well.
A whole \fBauthorized_keys\fR line may be pasted, and the key type is read from the key.
.TP
.B key_type \fItoken\fR \fIkey_type\fR
The kind of key: \fBrsa\fR, \fBed25519\fR or \fBecdsa\fR.
.TP
.B dump_sign_key \fItoken\fR / \fBdump_encrypt_key \fItoken\fR
Print one of the two public keys.
.TP
.B dump_private_key_backup \fItoken\fR
Print the encrypted backup of the private keys, if there is one.
This is what
.B otpme\-token deploy \-\-restore
reads to put the keys on a new card.
See
.B Deploy \- YubiKey PIV
above for where the backup comes from and what can read it.
.SS Signing a token
A signature says that somebody vouches for this token.
What is signed is the token, not something the token signs: the signature is made with the sign key of whoever is logged in, and it covers the data of the token \(em its public keys among it.
.PP
That is what a host asks for.
A host with token signers lets an SSH key in only when its token carries a signature from one of them, and a token with no signature at all is skipped.
Which host wants that is set with
.B otpme\-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type token
on the host, see
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.PP
\fBssh\fR and \fByubikey_piv\fR tokens take these:
.TP
.B sign \fR[\fB\-\-tags\fR \fItag1,tag2\fR] \fItoken\fR
Sign the token.
You have to be logged in with a key of your own, and that key makes the signature.
.TP
.B resign \fR[\fB\-\-tags\fR \fItag1,tag2\fR] \fItoken\fR
Sign it again, over the data as it is now.
For after something a signature covers has changed.
.TP
.B verify_sign \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fItoken\fR
Check the signatures of the token.
.TP
.B get_sign \fItoken\fR
Show them.
.TP
.B get_sign_data \fR[\fB\-\-tags\fR \fItag1,tag2\fR] \fItoken\fR
The bytes that have to be signed, for signing somewhere else.
.TP
.B add_sign \fItoken\fR \fIsignature\fR
Add a signature that was made somewhere else, from those bytes.
.TP
.B del_sign \fItoken\fR \fIsignature\fR
Remove a signature.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option belongs to one command and is described with that command above.
A short option means what the command it is given to makes of it: \fB\-r\fR replaces for
.B add
and
.BR move ,
and asks for the roles of roles for
.BR find_roles .
.PP
One option is not a command's own:
.TP
.B \-\-type \fItype\fR
Which type of token.
.B add
needs it, and so does every command under
.B TOKEN TYPE COMMANDS
\(em the type decides which commands there are at all.
.br
It goes in front of the command, not after it: OTPme reads the type before it parses the rest of the line.
.PP
Global options work with every command, see
.BR otpme (1).
.SH CONFIG PARAMETERS
Parameters are set on a token with
.B config
and shown with
.BR show_config .
.B get_config
answers what applies to the token once the user, the unit and the site have been looked at as well.
.PP
Which parameters there are, and which object types each of them can be set on, is in
.BR otpme (7).
.SH EXAMPLES
A token is named \fIuser\fB/\fIname\fR, and the name is yours to pick.
The examples below use the user \fBalice\fR.
.SS Giving somebody a token
.TP
.B otpme-token \-\-type yubikey_piv deploy alice/piv
A YubiKey, with a sign key and an encrypt key on the card.
This is the type OTPme recommends.
Needs the card in the machine you run it on.
.TP
.B otpme-token \-\-type fido2 deploy alice/key
A security key that signs a challenge.
Also needs the key in the machine.
.TP
.B otpme-token \-\-type totp add alice/phone
A TOTP token for an authenticator app.
It prints a QR code to scan; \fB\-\-no\-qrcode\fR leaves it out.
.TP
.B otpme-token \-\-type totp gen_qrcode alice/phone
The QR code again, for a second phone or a scan that went wrong.
.SS Letting the user set up their own
.TP
.B otpme-token \-\-type password add alice/start
A password token.
OTPme makes the password up and prints it; \fB\-\-password\fR sets one of your own.
Something has to get the user in the first time.
.TP
.B otpme-token enable_sso_deploy alice/start fido2
At their first login on the SSO portal, alice registers a security key for herself.
Then the password token can go.
.SS Helping somebody out
.TP
.B otpme-token temp_password \-\-generate \-\-duration 8h alice/login
A second password that works for the rest of the day, next to the one alice has.
For the support who has to set up her notebook.
.TP
.B otpme-token temp_password \-\-remove alice/login
Take it away before the day is over.
.TP
.B otpme-token \-\-type hotp resync alice/hardtoken
The counter of a hardware token ran ahead of what OTPme holds, because somebody pressed the button.
One OTP of it is enough to find the counter again.
.TP
.B otpme-token test alice/phone
Ask the token to prove itself.
It is the real check, so the OTP you type is spent.
.SS An SSH key the hosts accept
.TP
.B otpme-token \-\-type ssh add alice/ssh 'ssh\-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza...'
Add the public key of alice.
.TP
.B otpme-token \-\-type ssh sign alice/ssh
Sign the token with your own key.
A host that has token signers only lets the key in with this, see
.B Signing a token
above.
.TP
.B otpme-token find_hosts alice/ssh
Which hosts the token is assigned to, without following its roles.
.SS Before you throw one away
.TP
.B otpme-token find_roles \-r alice/phone
Which roles the token is in, and the roles those are in.
.TP
.B otpme-token find_shares alice/piv
Which shares it opens.
This one follows the roles, there is nothing to switch.
.TP
.B otpme-token del alice/phone
Delete it.
It goes to the trash and
.BR otpme\-trash (1)
can bring it back.
.SS Moving one
.TP
.B otpme-token move alice/phone bob
Give the token to bob, under the same name.
.TP
.B otpme-token move alice/phone TOKENSTORE
Park it, with nobody using it, under the name \fBalice:phone\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-token \-\-type totp add \-r alice/phone
Set the token up from scratch, with a new secret, and keep it in every role, group and ACL it is in.
The old one goes to the trash.
.br
A token is held by its UUID, and \fB\-r\fR gives the new token the UUID of the old one.
Deleting it and adding it again would give it a new UUID, and every assignment would be gone.
.br
alice has to scan the new QR code.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/otpme/
The objects of the realm, on a node.
A token lives under the user it belongs to.
.TP
.I /var/cache/otpme/offline/\fIuser_uuid\fI/
The tokens a host can check while it cannot reach the realm, one directory per user.
Written at login, and only for a token that allows it \(em see \fBOffline Configuration\fR above.
.TP
.I /var/cache/otpme/ssh/authorized_keys/\fIuser\fI/authorized_keys
The keys of the SSH tokens of that user, on a host, as sshd reads them.
Written by \fBhostd\fR from the tokens of the realm.
